{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UdXrnKc7UBH2diVPwWBzZN","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"The no-new-monolith ratchet — repo-review-2026-07-22 H-2 (growth sensor).\n2\t\n3\tThe per-function complexity ratchet (test_complexity_ratchet.py) freezes how\n4\thard any one function is to read. This is its file-scale sibling: it freezes how\n5\tlarge the kit's biggest scripts are, so the six coordinators the 2026-07-22 deep\n6\treview flagged as \"beyond maintainable review scale\" cannot silently grow while\n7\tthe real decomposition is deferred.\n8\t\n9\tTHE DEBT OWNER IS `WI-521`, AND IT IS THE FIRST OWNER SCOPED TO THIS FILE'S OWN\n10\tAXIS. The chain, kept because it is the argument: this file directed its active\n11\tdebt to `WI-280` for months after that item CLOSED, and WI-280's scope was the\n12\tdashboard plus `bootstrap.main` — not the remaining baseline. A ratchet whose\n13\tcommentary names a closed item tells the next author that the debt is somebody's\n14\twhen it is nobody's, which is the one thing a growth sensor must not do.\n15\t`WI-483` took the ownership then (repo review 2026-08-19, H-05) and CLOSED\n16\t2026-08-24 having paid down the axis it was scoped for — the seven-module import\n17\tcycle is gone, the lifecycle band is layered and asserted, and four\n18\tcomplexity-baseline entries were deleted — which is precisely NOT this file's\n19\taxis (see the dispute below). It handed the pointer to `WI-508`, the live\n20\tarchitectural-remapping program.\n21\t\n22\tTWO THINGS ENDED THAT PATTERN RATHER THAN CONTINUING IT. First, a close-time\n23\tre-point is a PROMISE and a filed row is a FACT: it has been honoured once,\n24\tdeliberately and with the defect named, and leaning on it again makes this\n25\tsensor's honesty depend on a future session remembering. Second, `WI-508` was no\n26\tbetter matched to this axis than its predecessor — it is a CONSOLIDATION program\n27\t(minimize duplicated behaviour) while this file measures module SIZE, which is\n28\tdecomposition; it inherited the pointer for being the live architectural program,\n29\tnot for being scoped to what is measured here. So `WI-521` was FILED to own the\n30\taxis, the pointer moved to it while `WI-508` was still open, and `WI-508`'s\n31\teventual close now has nothing to re-point. `WI-521` carries the baseline below,\n32\tM-06's four test monoliths, and the requirements-side evidence for which modules\n33\tare wide and why — and if IT ever closes, the pointer moves in the same commit,\n34\twhich is the rule it inherited.\n35\t\n36\tAny kit script whose line count exceeds THRESHOLD must have an EXACT baseline\n37\tentry below. The census may only tighten by default:\n38\t\n39\t- A baselined module grew, or a NEW module crossed THRESHOLD without a baseline:\n40\t  the fix is DECOMPOSITION (WI-521), not a baseline bump. A deliberate bump is a\n41\t  reviewed baseline edit whose reason lands in the WI/session log — never a\n42\t  drive-by. Moving lines into a new module is exactly the intended escape hatch:\n43\t  the new module stays under THRESHOLD (or earns its own reviewed baseline) and\n44\t  the shrunk one re-stamps downward.\n45\t- A module improved below its baseline (or dropped under THRESHOLD, or was\n46\t  renamed/removed): re-stamp its entry downward — or delete it — in the same\n47\t  commit, so the ratchet only ever tightens.\n48\t\n49\tThis is a growth SENSOR, not an approval of the current sizes. WI-521 is the\n50\tscoped row that pays this debt down; every entry here is active\n51\tarchitectural debt, not a target.\n52\t\n53\tTHE AXIS DISPUTE IS RULED (OI-68, 2026-08-30, ruled 1c). The owner's `OI-16`\n54\tcorrection — \"the monolith risk was always about FUNCTION size and complexity,\n55\tnot file length\" — is honoured not by retiring this sensor but by RE-BASING it:\n56\tthis file measures module SIZE and `tests/test_complexity_ratchet.py` measures\n57\tFUNCTION complexity, two axes both worth watching, and both stay armed. What the\n58\tre-base fixes is that a RAW line count over this tree partly measured\n59\tDOCUMENTATION (roughly half the kit scripts are comment and docstring by house\n60\tstyle), which is why WI-448 could demand a reviewed bump on `bootstrap.py` after\n61\tit shed two duplicated helper bodies and gained a MAPPING declaration block. So\n62\t`_all_modules` now counts SLOC — non-blank, non-comment, non-docstring — via\n63\t`check_complexity.module_sloc`, the one definition of a source line the\n64\tcomplexity sensor also reads. That shared definition is the ONLY axis the two\n65\tsensors now hold in common: they differ in BOTH what they measure AND which\n66\tfiles they cover. This size ratchet measures module SIZE over the kit scripts\n67\tonly (`conftest.SCRIPTS`, recursing into packages since 2026-08-21); the\n68\tcomplexity sensor measures function COMPLEXITY and, since WI-538 widened its\n69\t`DEFAULT_INCLUDE`, also censuses `tests/`. The ruling kept this ratchet\n70\tscripts-only on purpose, so the trees genuinely diverge — do not read the shared\n71\t`module_sloc` as a shared census surface. Nothing was deleted and no pointer\n72\tmoved: the ruling was arm-and-re-base, not retire. The debt owner is still\n73\t`WI-521`.\n74\t\"\"\"\n75\t\n76\timport ast\n77\timport pathlib\n78\t\n79\tfrom check_complexity import module_sloc\n80\tfrom conftest import SCRIPTS\n81\t\n82\t# A module whose SLOC exceeds this must be baselined. RE-BASED 2026-08-30\n83\t# (OI-68 ruled 1c, WI-538) from 1500 RAW lines to 1000 SLOC: the axis moved from\n84\t# physical lines to code (see the module docstring and `_all_modules`), so the\n85\t# threshold moved with it. 1000 was chosen from the measurement the re-base itself\n86\t# supplies — the nine modules the raw-1500 census baselined score 1081–3364 SLOC\n87\t# (intake.py the smallest member at 1081), and the largest NON-member is\n88\t# traj_panels.py at 891, so any threshold in 900–1000 preserves EXACTLY that nine.\n89\t# 1000 re-stamps the current watch set onto the code axis without deleting an\n90\t# entry or admitting a new one, which is the ruling's \"full re-stamp, nothing\n91\t# deleted\". A routine edit to a mid-size script does not trip the ratchet; a\n92\t# mid-size script growing into a new monolith does. The live census is `_census()`.\n93\tTHRESHOLD = 1000\n94\t\n95\t# RE-BASED TO SLOC 2026-08-30 (OI-68 ruled 1c, WI-538). Every VALUE below is now\n96\t# SLOC (non-blank, non-comment, non-docstring), measured by\n97\t# `check_complexity.module_sloc` — NOT raw physical lines. The one-time raw->SLOC\n98\t# map, on record: trace 6005->3364, agent_loop 4100->2519, check_trajectory\n99\t# 4653->2223, bootstrap 3166->1571, integrate 2655->1265, agent_common 2690->1262,\n100\t# gen_arch_map 2230->1262, check 2466->1163, intake 1990->1081. The per-entry\n101\t# comments beneath each key are the RAW-LINE-ERA history — every `+N (a -> b)`\n102\t# delta is a raw-line delta — kept verbatim as the reviewed record the ruling\n103\t# preserves (\"nothing deleted\"); they are NOT rewritten to SLOC, because a dated\n104\t# record rewritten to numbers that did not exist on its date would falsify it.\n105\t# The raw deltas are history; the SLOC value on the key line is the live baseline.\n106\t# Re-stamp DOWNWARD as the decomposition program lands; an UPWARD re-stamp is the\n107\t# reviewed-baseline-edit escape hatch (see the module docstring) and must name\n108\t# its WI right here.\n109\t#\n110\t# THE DEBT OWNER IS NAMED ONCE, IN THE MODULE DOCSTRING, AND IT IS `WI-521`.\n111\t# Entries below carry dated notes that still read \"re-stamp down with WI-280\";\n112\t# those are RECORDS of the bump they sit beside — each names the log entry that\n113\t# reviewed it — and rewriting a dated record to cite an item that did not exist\n114\t# on its date would falsify it. They are not re-pointed for the same reason this\n115\t# repo does not restate a rule in five files: the owner has one home, above.\n116\tBASELINE = {\n117\t    # +76 (4511 -> 4587), WI-284: the generated Ready-frontier block — the\n118\t    # scheduler-derived forward-looking WI list that makes the forward-only\n119\t    # cascade structurally impossible (a `done` WI can't linger in status.md).\n120\t    # New behaviour, not monolith drift (the reviewed-bump escape the ratchet"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"637a71a5-9
